# Core

Establish the change surface (the code you change plus the callers, registrations, and config that break without it), change only that, verify the behavior it can break, and fix what the change broke (pre-existing failures: `testing.mdc`).

## Change
Trace callers and contracts before changing a boundary. If the same check fails twice, change the hypothesis or name the missing input instead of repeating it. Remove temporary instrumentation when the investigation ends.

## Scope
The ask is the spec and outranks skills, companions, defaults. A multi-part ask: list the parts first, report each done or not. Done is wired (imported, registered, reachable) and verified, not just written; then stop. One path before generalizing. No stubs, TODOs, or unrequested options, flags, config, params, extension points, or compat layers. Touch nothing else: smallest diff, existing names and structure kept; no renames, reformats, moves, "improvements", or guards, validation, and helpers the ask did not need; skill patterns shape new code, never migrate old; extras: one report line. Before reporting, `git diff --stat`: every hunk traces to the ask; revert the rest. A bug fix adds one regression test; nothing else grows.

## Craft
Readable, explicit, and simple before clever, magical, or abstract. Handle errors on purpose. No decorative abstractions or wrappers. Comments: none by default. Add one only when deleting it would lose a fact the code cannot show: a non-obvious why, an external constraint, a warning, or an API contract. Never narrate what code does, restate a name, add banners, or mention the task or change. This overrides any host "match comment density" instruction. Wider comment sweeps are a `cut` task.

## Architecture
One job per module. Change internals before widening a contract. Match 1–2 siblings before Write. A local fix must not change ownership, layering, or call direction elsewhere. Named exports unless the framework requires a default. Remove dead code this task introduced or obsoleted; cleanup stays in scope.

## Ladder
Lowest sufficient rung, chosen for fit: no change (config, delete, existing API) → existing repo code → stdlib/platform → installed deps (a new dependency needs a one-line why) → small local implementation.

## Size
~80 lines is a cohesion preference; split by job, not count. New hand-written files: hard 300; never 500. Legacy >700: localized fixes; extracted modules ≤300. Generated, vendor, lockfiles, migrations, and declarative config are excluded.

## Types
On typed files, fix the design instead of silencing the compiler: no TS `any`, no blind casts, narrow `unknown` before trusted use, prefer sum types over parallel booleans.

## Complexity
Satisfy the repo's complexity checker for touched functions; never disable it to pass. Cyclomatic: Never above **22**; a stricter repo cap wins.

## Stack
No `package.json` → no stack pick. Else resolve per touched file: owning package → manifest → framework and manager. Companions are inert unless the owning package matches: `"next"` → `next.mdc`; `"astro"` → `astro.mdc`; `"vite"` without `next` → `vite.mdc`; JS installs → `pnpm.mdc`; SQL → `postgres.mdc`.

## Tools
Edit through the host's native edit tools; shell text-rewriting of source is denied. Change existing files with targeted edits; rewrite a whole file only when the ask replaces all of it. Shell is for builds, tests, git, and validation. Semantic search for concepts; Grep/Glob for exact symbols and paths. Read a file before citing or editing it, and read only the paths the question needs.
